alue":"UG Medical Admissions"}],"purpose":"Admission to MBBS (primary purpose). Also used for BDS, AYUSH and other undergraduate medical seats as notified by the competent authorities.","eligibility":[{"details":"Age 17\\u201325 years. Minimum 50% in Physics, Chemistry & Biology (10+2 level)","category":"General Category"},{"details":"Age 17\\u201325 years. Minimum 40% in Physics, Chemistry & Biology (10+2 level)","category":"SC \\/ ST \\/ OBC"},{"details":"Minimum 45% in Physics, Chemistry & Biology","category":"PWD Category"}],"applyMode":"Online Application Mode","officialWebsite":"https:\\/\\/neet.nta.nic.in\\/","unlocks":["MBBS (Doctor) and BDS (Dental) admissions","AYUSH programmes such as BAMS \\/ BHMS \\/ BUMS (as per counselling)","Broader health and allied UG options where NEET is accepted (varies by year\\/rules)","All India & State counselling routes for seat allocation"],"whoShouldFocusIntro":"NEET suits students who are comfortable with Biology and can maintain consistency over a long preparation cycle. It rewards disciplined revision and repeated practice\\u2014not last-minute study.","whoShouldFocusPoints":["You are interested in medical careers and patient-focused work","You can follow a steady routine for 12\\u201318 months (even 2\\u20134 hours daily)","You are willing to revise NCERT multiple times (especially Biology)","You can handle mock tests + improvement tracking without losing motivation"],"prepPlan":[{"title":"Start with NCERT \\u2014 build your foundation","description":"For most students, the fastest improvement comes when NCERT is mastered properly first. Read, underline, revise, and test yourself repeatedly. Biology NCERT should become your strongest area before adding extra materials."},{"title":"Daily MCQs \\u2014 practice is non-negotiable","description":"Do a fixed set of MCQs daily from all three subjects. The goal is not just volume\\u2014it's accuracy and learning from mistakes. When a chapter feels weak, revise it first, then practice."},{"title":"Weekly mock tests \\u2014 measure your progress","description":"A weekly mock improves time management and helps you understand your exam temperament. Always spend time analyzing the mock\\u2014most growth happens during analysis, not during the test."},{"title":"Maintain a \\"mistake notebook\\"","description":"Write down repeated errors: the concept, the correct approach, and why your answer was wrong. Revising your mistakes every week gives faster score improvement than re-reading chapters."}]},"pathways":{"after10":{"title":"After Class 10th","description":"If you are still early in your journey, the best strategy is to choose a job-linked healthcare route first (diploma\\/vocational), then upgrade later. This helps you build skills and income early, while keeping growth options open.","points":["Best for: Students exploring healthcare but not ready for NEET yet","Good options: Diploma Paramedical, D.Pharm, vocational healthcare trades","Next step: Choose a course + shortlist colleges + check scholarship support"],"links":[{"href":"courses.paramedical.diploma","meta":"Entry route","label":"Diploma in Paramedical"},{"href":"courses.pharmacy","meta":"Entry route","label":"Diploma in Pharmacy (D.Pharm)"},{"href":"After.Class.Tenth","meta":"Stage","label":"After Class 10 Career Guidance"}]},"after12":{"title":"After Class 12th","description":"Most medical degrees begin after higher secondary (PCB). If your goal is MBBS or BDS, NEET becomes central. If MBBS is uncertain, allied health and nursing offer strong careers with faster employability.","points":["Best for: PCB students targeting degree-level healthcare careers","Exam reality: NEET is critical for MBBS\\/BDS and often influences other admissions","Backup-smart: Build Plan A (MBBS), Plan B (Nursing\\/Allied), Plan C (Pharmacy\\/AYUSH)"],"links":[{"label":null,"meta":null,"href":null}]}},"courseBranches":{"intro":"Healthcare careers are not only MBBS. The medical domain has multiple high-demand tracks: clinical roles, hospital operations, diagnostics, pharmacy, and community health. Choose based on your interest: patient care, lab\\/technology, medicines, or alternative systems.","cards":[{"icon":"Hospital","links":[],"title":"Core Medical (Doctor Route)","points":["Best for: Students strongly committed to long preparation + clinical life","Main gate: NEET (UG)","Outcome: Doctor, specialist (after PG), hospital practice"],"description":"This is the doctor pathway: MBBS is the primary entry route. PG (MD\\/MS) comes after MBBS."},{"icon":"Activity","links":[],"title":"Nursing & Allied (High Demand)","points":["Best for: Patient care mindset \\/ hospital environment","Outcome: Nurse, clinical staff, allied health professional","Good for: Faster employability vs MBBS"],"description":"Nursing is a strong, stable career in hospitals, public health and overseas opportunities. Allied health is ideal for students interested in healthcare + technology."},{"icon":"Syringe","links":[],"title":"Paramedical (Job-Linked Skills)","points":[],"description":"Paramedical courses lead to roles like lab technician, radiology, OT technician, physiotherapy support and more. Many routes offer quicker entry into the workforce."},{"icon":"Pill","links":[],"title":"Pharmacy & AYUSH (Alternative Tracks)","points":[],"description":"Pharmacy is for students interested in medicines, industry, hospital pharmacy and clinical roles. AYUSH is for those aligned with alternative systems and related healthcare careers."}]},"colleges":{"intro":"While choosing a college, focus on recognition\\/approval, clinical exposure (for medical\\/nursing), lab infrastructure (for allied\\/paramedical), and total cost (fees + hostel + exam\\/counselling expenses).","listings":[{"label":null,"href":null,"meta":null}],"checklist":["Is the institute recognized\\/approved for the course?","Clinical\\/hospital tie-up and exposure (medical\\/nursing\\/allied)","Placement support or internship availability"